Today, in the presence of Hon’ble Agriculture Minister Shri Pijush Hazarika, the Government of Assam formalised an MoU with the International Food Policy Research Institute (IFPRI), represented by Shri T. Nandakumar, Former Secretary, Government of India; Dr. Avinash Kishore, Acting Director for South Asia, IFPRI; and other senior representatives from IFPRI.

The partnership will bring research, evidence and global expertise closer to Assam’s agricultural priorities, with focus on climate resilience, flood mitigation, agricultural diversification and rural livelihoods.

Given Assam’s exposure to floods and climate risks, the collaboration will support practical, evidence-based approaches to resilient agriculture, diversification of rice-based farming systems into pulses, oilseeds and horticulture, and development of value chains across tea, horticulture, livestock and fisheries.

Assam’s large community of small tea growers offers an important opportunity to develop context-specific models for climate resilience and livelihood enhancement that can be tested, refined and scaled through State programmes.

The MoU will also strengthen institutional capacity in data analytics, policy modelling, programme evaluation and impact assessment, supported by household-level data and longitudinal surveys to better understand farm incomes, risks, vulnerabilities and nutrition outcomes.

With more than 2 lakh personnel recruited by the State in recent years against a workforce of around 4 lakh, strengthening contemporary technical capabilities within government institutions remains an important priority.

Assam already has substantial experience with international development institutions, with large number of externally aided projects currently in progress and a significant size of portfolio. The focus now should be on translating research and evidence into measurable interventions that can be integrated into government programmes and taken to scale.

